  "summary": "When a stream is read in unordered mode (for example through `RecvStream::read(false)` in",
  "details": "When a stream is read in unordered mode (for example through `RecvStream::read(false)` in\nquinn-proto or `read_unordered` in `quinn`), quinn-proto proactively deduplicates received data\nby recording received ranges in a `RangeSet` through its `replace` method. When an incoming\nSTREAM frame carried an empty payload, the `Drop` implementation of the iterator returned by\n`replace` inserted an empty range into the set, violating the invariant that stored ranges are\nnon-empty. Subsequent frames overlapping that region then caused a panic while processing the\nstream.\n\nA remote peer can trigger this with five small STREAM frames (two of them zero-length) once the\napplication has put the stream in unordered-read mode. The default ordered read path is not\naffected.\n\nThe flaw was corrected in 0.11.18 by ignoring empty ranges in `RangeSet::replace`.\n\nThanks to Otto Hermann ([@ojhermann](https://github.com/ojhermann)) for reporting this issue.",
  "affected": [
